course: reflection
lesson: 28
title: Reading people in hard times: what's really behind the mean face
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: For weeks Kyle's mate Dev kept snapping at him, "Leave it, man, just leave it," and Kyle lay in bed thinking: "What did I do? He's turned into a proper muppet." ("Muppet" is UK slang for an idiot.) He was angry and hurt, and he stopped calling round.
explain: Then Dev's mum told Kyle's mum that Dev's dad had moved out; Kyle felt shocked, then guilty for being so cross; over the next month he just sat with Dev, played games without asking questions, said "I'm here if you want to talk", and slowly Dev talked again.
explain: What Kyle understood: when someone suddenly turns mean, it is often because of something hard happening to them that we cannot see; the snapping was not really about Kyle; it does not make it okay, but understanding it changes what you do next.
explain: People hide hard times in many ways: "I'm fine" with red eyes, "I don't care!" with a slammed door, "Whatever" with a stomach ache, laughing a lot while not eating lunch; the words say one thing, and the face, the habits and the changes say another.
explain: Reading these clues is not about guessing someone's secrets; it is about not taking every bad mood personally, asking kindly, and telling a trusted adult when a friend seems to be struggling.
fact: When someone suddenly acts mean or snappy, it is often because of something hard in their own life; "taking it out on you" means letting your bad feelings land on someone else.
fact: Understanding why someone is mean does not make the meanness okay, but it can change how you respond.
fact: Sitting with a struggling friend, without pushing for answers, and saying "I'm here if you want to talk" often helps more than questions.
fact: Grown-ups sometimes say "I'm fine" to protect children from worry; their faces often show the truth, and a hug or "you don't have to be fine" can help.
fact: People's moods are often about things in their own lives, like a teacher whose pet has just died; not every bad mood is about you.
fact: People sometimes laugh by reflex when someone falls; a real friend stops and helps when they see you are hurt.
fact: Being quiet or withdrawn can mean sad or worried, not angry; asking is better than guessing.
fact: "Hurt people hurt people" is a saying that people in pain sometimes hurt others; it explains but does not excuse, and you can be kind while still saying "stop being mean".
fact: "I don't care!" often means "I care too much to talk about it right now"; giving space and coming back later helps.
fact: Short answers like "whatever" can be about how someone feels inside, not about you.
fact: "Boys don't cry" is an old saying; crying is not weak, and older people sometimes grew up with different rules.
fact: Masking means hiding real feelings behind a brave face, for example by laughing a lot; small changes, like not eating, can be clues, and telling a trusted adult is the right step.
exercise: Dev snapped at Kyle for weeks. What was really behind it? | His dad had moved out, and he was struggling | Kyle had done something terrible to him that he didn't remember | Dev had decided he hated Kyle forever | Dev wanted a new best friend instead
exercise: Does understanding Dev's reason make the snapping okay? | No, but it changes how Kyle responds | Yes, a reason excuses anything at all | No, so Kyle should snap back harder | Yes, and Kyle must say sorry for it
exercise: What helped Dev talk again? | Kyle just being around and saying "I'm here" | Kyle asking him lots of questions every day | Kyle snapping back at him for weeks | Kyle telling everyone about Dev's dad
exercise: Lily's mum says "I'm fine, sweetheart" but her eyes are red. Why might she say it? | To protect Lily from worry | Because she wants Lily to go away | Because she is lying to be mean | Because red eyes mean she is happy
exercise: What helped Lily and her mum? | A hug and "You don't have to be fine" | Pretending she didn't notice at all | Asking Dad what the fight was about | Crying even louder than her mum so she'd notice
exercise: Josh's teacher was cross with him, and later he learned her dog had died that day. What is the lesson? | Not every bad mood is about you | Teachers are always cross for no reason | Josh must have caused it somehow | People should never be in a bad mood
exercise: Mia's friend laughed when Mia tripped, then saw her crying and stopped. What is true? | People sometimes laugh by reflex; a real friend stops and helps | She laughed, so she can never ever be a real friend to Mia again | Laughing at someone falling is always planned | Mia should laugh at her friend next time
exercise: Tom's dad went very quiet after losing his job. Tom thinks Dad is angry with him. What helped? | Asking Dad, who said he was worried about work | Guessing every night in bed what Dad was thinking about him | Staying out of Dad's way for months | Making lots of noise to cheer him up
exercise: Kira is mean to everyone, and her mum is very ill. What is the saying for this? | Hurt people hurt people | Mean people are born mean | Sad people are always kind | Everyone's mean on Mondays
exercise: What did Holly do about Kira? | Was kind, but still told her to stop being mean | Was mean back so Kira knew how it felt | Let Kira carry on being mean because her mum is so ill | Told the whole class about Kira's mum
exercise: Leo's brother lost his final, shouted "I don't care!" and slammed his door. What did he mean? | He cared too much to talk right then | He truly didn't care about the final | He was angry at Leo for something | He wanted to quit football that day
exercise: What helped Leo's brother? | Leaving him a while, then asking to play FIFA | Teasing him about the final through the door | Knocking every minute until he came out | Telling him through the door that he had played really badly
exercise: Ruby's friend said "Whatever" and walked off. Next day she said she had a stomach ache. What is the lesson? | Short answers can be about how someone feels inside | "Whatever" always means that someone is angry with you | Ruby should never talk to her again | Stomach aches make people angry at friends
exercise: Archie's grandad said "Boys don't cry" when Archie cried at the funeral. What is true? | Crying isn't weak | Boys really must never cry | Only grandads are allowed to cry | Crying at funerals is rude
exercise: Ellie's friend laughs a lot since her parents split, but doesn't eat lunch. What is this called? | Masking | Showing off | Being totally fine | Dieting
exercise: What should Ellie do? | Tell a teacher she is worried about her friend | Ignore it, since her friend keeps saying she is fine and laughing | Tell everyone in class what she noticed | Stop eating lunch too to keep her company
test: Kyle's brother goes, "Dev's being a right muppet, just ghost him." ("Ghost" means stop talking to someone without saying why.) What's the wiser view? | Something hard may be going on; stay around and be kind | His brother is right; ghosting fixes everything | Kyle should snap at Dev even harder | Kyle should demand Dev says sorry first
test: What would probably have happened if Kyle kept snapping back for months? | The friendship would likely have broken while Dev struggled alone | Dev would have told him everything straight away | Dev's dad would have moved back home and everything would be normal | Nothing would change at all
test: Lily's gran from Aberdeen says, "Yer mam's no fine, quine, gie her a cuddle." ("Quine" means girl; "gie" means give.) What's the gran noticing? | Mum's face shows she isn't fine, and a hug could help | Mum is lying to Lily to be mean | Lily should sit Mum down and ask her all about the fight with Dad | Mum wants to be left alone forever
test: Josh says, "She was well grumpy, she must hate me." Later he hears about her dog. What helped Josh? | Drawing her a card | Being rude back to her | Telling her she was unfair | Asking for a new teacher
test: Mia's mate says, "she laughed when you fell, she's fake, drop her." What's fairer? | People sometimes laugh by reflex; she said sorry | Anyone who laughs once is fake forever | Mia should drop all her friends to be safe | Laughing at a fall is always cruel on purpose
test: Tom's mum, from Texas, says, "Your daddy's just fixin' to figure out work, darlin'." ("Fixin' to" means about to, trying to.) What does Tom learn? | Dad's quiet was about worry, not anger at Tom | Dad was angry with Tom after all | Tom should fix Dad's work for him | Quiet people are always secretly angry inside about something
test: Holly's mate goes, "Kira's mum is ill, so let her be mean, it's fine." What's the better balance? | Be kind to Kira, but still tell her to stop being mean | Let Kira be mean because she's sad | Be mean back so Kira learns her lesson | Tell everyone so they all avoid Kira
test: Leo's brother says, "I don't care!" In a cartoon, a character says that and the story moves on. What's often real? | Saying "I don't care" can hide caring a lot | People who say it truly never care | Slammed doors mean someone is happy | It means he really wants everyone to come into his room
test: Ruby lies awake: "She said whatever, she's mad at me." What would have saved her a night of worry? | Asking her friend the next day | Worrying harder to prepare herself | Sending her friend angry messages | Telling the class her friend was rude
test: Archie's mum says, "Big boys cry too, love, your grandad grew up with different rules." What is she telling him? | Crying is not weak, whatever the old saying | Grandad was right and Archie is weak | Archie should never ever cry anywhere near his grandad again | Only adults are allowed to cry
test: Ellie's friend goes, "I'm fine, lol, it's all good, no stress." But she hasn't eaten lunch all week. What should Ellie do? | Tell a teacher she's worried | Believe the "lol" and forget it | Laugh along so it's all fine | Wait to see if it gets worse
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a sudden change towards unkind conduct in a friend? | It frequently arises from undisclosed difficulties in the friend's own life | It invariably reflects a deliberate decision to end the friendship | It is wholly attributable to the conduct of the recipient | It is a reliable indication of an unkind character from birth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an adult may assert that she is "fine" whilst visibly distressed. | To protect a child from anxiety | To deceive the child for malicious ends | Because distress is a sign of contentment | Because adults are incapable of sadness
exam: It is given that a friend laughs frequently but has ceased to eat at lunchtime following a family upheaval. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The friend may be masking distress, and a trusted adult ought to be informed | The friend is evidently content and no action is warranted | The matter ought to be announced to the class | The friend's conduct is solely an attempt to attract attention
exam: In respect of the saying "hurt people hurt people", determine which statement is correct. | It explains unkind conduct without excusing it | It establishes that unkind conduct is wholly excused | It applies solely to persons who are physically injured | It requires that unkind conduct be reciprocated
